WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:07.504
Around the world and especially
in low-resource countries

00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:09.381
like my home country of Kenya,

00:00:09.422 --> 00:00:11.800
mental health problems
prevent young people

00:00:11.841 --> 00:00:14.886
from living independent
and successful lives.

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:18.890
I was born and raised
in a small sugarcane farm.

00:00:18.932 --> 00:00:22.310
Some of my early memories
are those of my younger brother and I

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.605
walking around and pretending
that we owned the sugarcane.

00:00:26.106 --> 00:00:29.609
But most of these memories
were actually from school.

00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:32.821
My parents and especially my mother,

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:35.115
made sure that I knew
how much they'd sacrificed

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:36.616
for me to have the education

00:00:36.658 --> 00:00:39.452
and the path to success that they did not.

00:00:39.953 --> 00:00:42.831
They were very clear
that the stakes were too high.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.417
There was no room for disappointment.

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.210
There was no room for failure.

00:00:48.086 --> 00:00:49.254
When I was 12,

00:00:49.254 --> 00:00:52.799
I moved to the boarding section
of a local primary school.

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:56.553
Here we will wake up at 4am
to a set of math problems

00:00:56.595 --> 00:00:58.930
that we had to solve before breakfast.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.974
We'd only go to bed, sometimes after 11,

00:01:01.016 --> 00:01:04.394
if we solved that night's
science problems.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.897
In high school,
the pressure was even higher.

00:01:08.398 --> 00:01:10.317
The early-morning to late-night routines

00:01:10.358 --> 00:01:13.820
were now supplemented
by Saturday and Sunday classes.

00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:16.406
It was here in high school

00:01:16.448 --> 00:01:18.742
where I began to notice the personal,

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:22.579
emotional and behavioral toll
that this pressure-cooker system

00:01:22.621 --> 00:01:25.415
had exerted of myself and those around me.

00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:28.168
I noticed it, for example,

00:01:28.209 --> 00:01:31.838
when tired of cramming
the production of sulfuric acid,

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.425
I stopped taking notes or engaging
with chemistry in 10th grade.

00:01:36.343 --> 00:01:39.095
I noticed it when my friend Onsongo

00:01:39.095 --> 00:01:42.182
tired that his life had been reduced
to a letter grade,

00:01:42.223 --> 00:01:43.933
had enough and quit school.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:48.271
I noticed it when a schoolmate,
whom I will call Otieno,

00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:49.731
took his own life.

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:54.027
But all this time,

00:01:54.027 --> 00:01:58.073
all this time, what came to mind
was that this was life.

00:01:59.032 --> 00:02:02.160
Life was trouble sleeping
or sleeping too much.

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:06.873
Life was little interest or pleasure
in doing the things that gave you joy.

00:02:07.332 --> 00:02:10.043
Life was constantly feeling down,

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:14.005
crushing under the weight
of the feelings of hopelessness.

00:02:15.423 --> 00:02:16.883
That was life.

00:02:17.884 --> 00:02:21.388
It was only recently,
some five years after high school,

00:02:21.388 --> 00:02:24.766
that I began to realize
that what we thought was life

00:02:24.766 --> 00:02:27.185
could have, in fact,
been mental health problems.

00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:31.272
Was it possible that we were struggling
with depression and anxiety

00:02:31.314 --> 00:02:32.816
and just didn't know about it?

00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:36.236
That, in fact, was the case.

00:02:36.778 --> 00:02:40.824
Studies now show that almost
50 percent of Kenyan adolescents

00:02:40.865 --> 00:02:42.951
struggle with mental health problems.

00:02:43.535 --> 00:02:45.495
And in such a youthful country,

00:02:45.537 --> 00:02:48.957
where half the population
is 19 years or younger,

00:02:48.998 --> 00:02:52.919
youth mental health has now become
an urgent public health priority.

00:02:55.714 --> 00:02:57.006
Over the past few years,

00:02:57.048 --> 00:02:59.509
I've been working to turn
this public health problem

00:02:59.509 --> 00:03:01.302
into an opportunity for young people

00:03:01.302 --> 00:03:04.597
to reimagine and redefine
what mental health care could look like.

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:07.642
We're doing this by tackling
the three big issues

00:03:07.642 --> 00:03:10.019
that prevent young people
from getting help.

00:03:10.353 --> 00:03:13.314
One, the lack of mental health experts.

00:03:13.314 --> 00:03:15.859
Two, the stigma around mental health.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.571
And three, the sad reality
that most mental health treatments

00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:21.823
were not built and designed
for young people

00:03:21.823 --> 00:03:24.284
who look like me and have my background.

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:30.457
Because we have only two clinicians
for every one million Kenyans,

00:03:30.498 --> 00:03:32.417
and two -- yes, only two --

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:36.337
child and adolescent psychiatrist
in the whole country,

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:42.093
we are expanding mental health care access
by training [18- to 22-year-old] Kenyans

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:43.762
as lay providers.

00:03:44.429 --> 00:03:49.100
These young Kenyans are delivering
evidence-based care to their peers

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.019
through a structured and tiered model

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.482
that ensures that all young people
who want can get help.

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:57.734
Because of a stigma of mental health

00:03:57.776 --> 00:04:00.528
that still exists from
Kenya's colonial past,

00:04:00.528 --> 00:04:02.030
we are delivering interventions

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.908
without a formal diagnosis
of mental health,

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:08.369
and our interventions focus
on building character strengths,

00:04:08.369 --> 00:04:10.580
on strengthening individual autonomy

00:04:10.622 --> 00:04:14.292
and on improving overall
human functioning and well-being.

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:18.546
Because our lay providers
work within the communities

00:04:18.588 --> 00:04:19.881
where they come from,

00:04:19.923 --> 00:04:22.509
in the school systems
that they themselves went to

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:27.555
and deliver what is truly a for-youth
and by-youth mental health care treatment,

00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:32.560
we are fixing the divide
between mental health treatment

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.190
and the social and cultural needs
of the communities that they serve.

00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:42.529
Since 2018, we have used
this youth-based model

00:04:42.529 --> 00:04:45.490
to bring mental health care across Kenya.

00:04:46.032 --> 00:04:49.536
And what we have found
is that this model works.

00:04:50.036 --> 00:04:54.666
Young people are reporting reductions
in depression and anxiety symptoms.

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:57.502
They’re reporting improvements
in their social relationships

00:04:57.502 --> 00:05:01.172
and in their views about their abilities
to change the world.

00:05:01.673 --> 00:05:03.508
And in what will make my mom happy,

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:07.345
they are also reporting improvements
in their academic grades.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:13.059
Mental health is not a Kenyan problem.

00:05:13.685 --> 00:05:15.812
It is not an African problem.

00:05:16.312 --> 00:05:18.231
This is a global problem.

00:05:18.273 --> 00:05:21.234
We need big ideas to tackle this problem.

00:05:21.734 --> 00:05:24.863
And our case for optimism
is that our youth-oriented,

00:05:24.904 --> 00:05:29.117
community-focused model can be a template
for the rest of the world.

00:05:30.702 --> 00:05:32.954
One other early memory from childhood

00:05:32.996 --> 00:05:35.582
is a story that my grandmother,
Yunita, told me.

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:39.627
A long time ago,
before the sugarcane farms,

00:05:39.627 --> 00:05:43.131
before the grass-thatched huts
and the round compounds,

00:05:43.172 --> 00:05:46.384
the animals lived happily
because there was plenty in the land

00:05:46.426 --> 00:05:48.303
and a future to look forward to.

00:05:49.429 --> 00:05:51.347
But farming threatened the land,

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:53.600
and the future was all of a sudden bleak.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.269
And so the lion roared.

00:05:56.936 --> 00:05:58.813
But there was no water.

00:05:58.855 --> 00:06:00.523
And the elephant grunted.

00:06:00.982 --> 00:06:02.442
But there was no water.

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:05.028
And the cheetah yelped,

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:06.404
but there was no water.

00:06:06.738 --> 00:06:09.866
But when the young antelope
asked them to come together

00:06:09.908 --> 00:06:12.952
and build a well, there was water.

00:06:13.494 --> 00:06:14.913
There was a future.

00:06:14.913 --> 00:06:16.122
There was hope.

00:06:16.998 --> 00:06:19.626
This is our young antelope moment.

00:06:20.084 --> 00:06:22.253
Let us come together
and let us build a world

00:06:22.295 --> 00:06:26.966
where all young people, all over the world
can flourish and thrive.

00:06:27.008 --> 00:06:28.217
Thank you.

00:06:28.217 --> 00:06:31.846
(Applause)

